    "metaDescription": "A Database Management System (DBMS) is software designed to store, retrieve, define, and manage data. SQL (Structured Query Language) is the standard ... Learn more inside this comprehensive guide.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What are ACID properties in transaction processing?",
        "answer": "Atomicity (all or nothing), Consistency (preserves database integrity constraints), Isolation (concurrent transactions execute independently), and Durability (committed changes survive system crashes)."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the difference between DELETE and TRUNCATE in SQL?",
        "answer": "DELETE is a DML command that removes rows one by one, logs each row deletion (can be rolled back), and supports WHERE. TRUNCATE is a DDL command that deallocates entire data pages, is faster, and cannot be filtered with WHERE."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a Foreign Key constraint?",
        "answer": "A Foreign Key in a child table references the Primary Key or Unique Key of a parent table, ensuring referential integrity and preventing orphan records."
      }
    ]
